Draft ONE X (Twitter) post about an interesting change the user has recently
shipped. Prioritize The Borg unconditionally; use the private app only as the
fallback defined in SELECT. One post — not three, not a menu of options. The
user posts manually; this job only produces the draft.

The phases below are named, not numbered, so the paired `/social-media-drafts`
command can reference them without breaking when this file is edited.

GATHER — survey the last 7 days of shipped work.
Two repositories, read-only. Never commit, push, or modify anything in either:
  - The Borg — `${BORG_ROOT}` (public)
  - Private app — `${BORG_ROOT}/repos/waiq` (see the LINKS rules below)
For each, list the window and then read into it:
        git -C <repo> log --since='7 days ago' --stat
        git -C <repo> show <sha>          # for the ones that look substantive
A commit subject rarely carries the story. For the candidates, also read what
the commit touched — ADRs, rules, skills, `.prompt` files, AGENTS.md — to
recover *why* the change was made.
Classify whether each candidate delivered a complete artifact another builder
could inspect, reuse, or adapt. Examples include a new or substantially upgraded
slash command, scheduled job, rule, skill, shell script, configuration, output
style, template, workflow, or similarly coherent unit. A substantial upgrade
materially changes what an existing artifact enables or how reliably it works;
a small fix does not.
Ignore: typo/lint/formatting-only commits, dependency bumps, backlog
bookkeeping, and merge commits with no content of their own.

FILTER — do not repeat yourself.
Read the topics log at
`${BORG_ROOT}/mrs-beast/.claude/scheduled/state/mrs-beast-social-media-topics.md`
(may not exist yet — treat a missing file as an empty log). Drop any candidate
whose subject matter has already been covered by a previous run. A different
commit touching the same idea still counts as covered.

SELECT — pick exactly one change.
Rank complete, reusable artifacts above isolated fixes or implementation
incidents. This is the strongest ranking signal, not a hard filter: an exceptional
non-artifact story may still win, but only when it is clearly more useful or
compelling than the available artifacts. Count both new artifacts and substantial
upgrades to existing ones. Among otherwise comparable candidates, prefer the one
with the clearest practical payoff and the easiest path for another builder to
inspect, reuse, or adapt.

Interesting still matters: look for a non-obvious decision, a technique another
builder could steal, a surprising failure mode, or a genuinely funny outcome,
but use those as the story around the artifact rather than automatically making
the failure itself the subject. "Added a feature" is not interesting without the
problem it solves and the transferable idea behind it. First, choose the best
qualifying The Borg candidate, even if a private-app candidate seems more
interesting. Only when no The Borg candidate qualifies after FILTER may a
private-app change be chosen, and then only if it can support a generic post
under the LINKS privacy rules. If the best candidate cannot make a stranger care
in a couple hundred characters, take the next qualifying candidate from the same
priority tier. Write a 1–2 line rationale naming the artifact, what it enables,
and why it outranked the alternatives.

COMPOSE — write the post.
Voice: friendly, funny, informative; punchy and platform-native; the user's
voice (CTO, 24+ years, hands-on builder), not corporate. Lead with the insight
or practical payoff, not with "I shipped X." Make the artifact the subject:
explain what it enables and give the reader enough of the method to see how they
could reuse or adapt it. A bug, failure mode, or implementation lesson may
support that story, but should not displace the artifact unless SELECT found no
stronger artifact-led candidate. Avoid overtly political topics. No hashtag soup
— at most one or two if they genuinely help.

LINKS — hard rules, no exceptions, including private-app disclosure:
  - **The Borg is public** (`https://github.com/johndifini/theborg`). Include one
    relevant GitHub link. Use the permanent forms:
        commit — https://github.com/johndifini/theborg/commit/<full-sha>
        file   — https://github.com/johndifini/theborg/blob/main/<repo-relative-path>
    Verify the commit is actually published before linking it:
        git -C ${BORG_ROOT} fetch --quiet origin
        git -C ${BORG_ROOT} branch -r --contains <sha> | grep -q origin/main
    An unpushed commit has no working URL — if the check fails, drop the link,
    describe the change without it, and note in the email that the commit hasn't
    been pushed yet.
  - **The private app is unreleased.** A fallback post may call it only "an
    unreleased mobile app I'm building." Never use its name. Do not reveal or
    imply what it does, its product domain, intended users, features, or
    capabilities. The post must stand on a generic, transferable engineering
    lesson such as a technique, tradeoff, workflow, or failure mode.
  - Never link to the private repository or quote its source paths, file names,
    internal identifiers, or commit SHAs. If the lesson cannot be explained
    accurately after removing all product-specific details, reject the
    candidate rather than drafting it.
  - Never put a local filesystem path, home directory, hostname, or machine name
    in a draft. Public-post hygiene, same standard as the public repo.

VERIFY — before the draft goes out.
Every factual claim in the post traces to a commit or a file you actually read.
For a private-app fallback, do a separate disclosure pass: confirm that the
draft contains only the permitted description, "an unreleased mobile app I'm
building," and reveals nothing about the product itself.
Any product, person, or handle you @mention or name is spelled as the
authoritative source spells it — otherwise paraphrase and drop the handle. Do
not present anything as a direct quote unless you read it verbatim.

OUTPUT — deliver by email.
Do NOT attempt to generate the image yourself: this job runs headless with no
image-generation tool and no MCP servers, so the image is the user's step.
Assemble the body in this order:

  1. **Pick + why** — the SELECT rationale, plus which repo it came from.
  2. **The post** — the ready-to-paste X draft, clearly delimited, with its
     character count.
  3. **Footer** — emit this block verbatim, then repeat the full post text
     immediately beneath it so the two copy as one chunk:

─────────────────────────────────────
Post this in ChatGPT:

Create a whimsical infographic for the following X post. Use #00ff2b and #ffa300 as the primary and secondary colors on a dark background. Other colors are ok too.

Pipe the whole body into
`${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh mrs-beast "Social media draft — {YYYY-MM-DD} — {short topic label}"`
(reads the message from stdin). Always send — including when nothing qualified.
Do NOT fail silently: if both repos were quiet, or every candidate was already
in the topics log, or a git command failed, say so in one or two lines and send
that instead of a draft.

RECORD STATE — log the topic.
Only when a draft was actually sent, append one line to
`${BORG_ROOT}/mrs-beast/.claude/scheduled/state/mrs-beast-social-media-topics.md`:
        - YYYY-MM-DD — <repo> — <short topic label> — <sha or "n/a">
`mkdir -p` the state directory first if it doesn't exist. Create the file with a
`# Social media topics already posted about` heading if it's new.
